course: religion-ethics
lesson: 82
title: Trust that breaks and takes long to mend
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Trust is like a glass bridge between two people: one lie can crack it in a second, but mending it takes many careful days.
explain: Tom said he was at the park but went to the river, and now Mum wants a text every time he arrives anywhere for a month; it feels harsh, but each text is one rung on a ladder back up.
explain: Forgiveness and trust are different: forgiving means letting go of anger and can happen quickly, while trust is relying on someone again and grows back slowly through kept promises.
explain: The Bible shows broken trust that was mended: Peter denied Jesus three times, wept, and was given new work by Jesus, "Feed my sheep" (John 21:15-17), and Jacob and Esau made peace after twenty years.
explain: The quickest road back is a full, honest apology with no "but", making amends in deeds, and being faithful in small things, day after day.
fact: Christians believe God forgives at once when we confess (1 John 1:9), but forgiveness is not the same as trust; trust grows back through steady faithfulness.
fact: An old saying goes: trust arrives on foot and leaves on horseback.
fact: Peter said three times that he did not know Jesus, then "went outside and wept bitterly" (Matthew 26:69-75; Luke 22:54-62).
fact: After the resurrection Jesus asked Peter three times, "Do you love me?" and three times gave him work: "Feed my sheep" (John 21:15-17); Peter became a leader of the church.
fact: One accident is forgivable, but a pattern of excuses, like "losing" a borrowed thing three times, becomes a trust problem.
fact: You can forgive fully and still be careful: Jesus taught to forgive "seventy-seven times" (Matthew 18:22) and to be "as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves" (Matthew 10:16).
fact: A small debt carries a big trust; Psalm 37:21 says, "The wicked borrow and do not repay, but the righteous give generously."
fact: Jacob tricked his blind father Isaac to take Esau's blessing (Genesis 27); he had to run away, was tricked in turn by his uncle Laban (Genesis 29), and years later Esau ran to hug him (Genesis 33:4).
fact: Proverbs 16:28 says, "A gossip separates close friends": passing on what you were told in trust is a kind of dishonesty.
fact: Proverbs 28:13: "Whoever conceals their sins does not prosper, but the one who confesses and renounces them finds mercy."
fact: Researchers say a good apology names the wrong, takes responsibility, shows you understand the hurt, offers to fix it and promises change, with no "but".
fact: Jesus told of two sons: one said "I will go" to the vineyard and didn't, the other said "I won't" and then went; the second did his father's will (Matthew 21:28-31).
fact: Zacchaeus told Jesus, "If I have cheated anybody out of anything, I will pay back four times the amount" (Luke 19:8-9): making amends in deeds earns trust back.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «sus» means «suspicious»; in Scotland «a quid» is a pound, «the morra» means «tomorrow» and «dinnae fash» means «don't worry»; «gutted» means very upset; in Australia a «bludger» is someone who lets others do the work, and «zip» means nothing.
fact: When Leah's dad was a boy he nicked (stole) sweets from Mr Patel's shop; Mr Patel let him work Saturdays to pay it back, and a year later trusted him with the till.
fact: Ananias and Sapphira kept part of the money from a field and pretended they had given it all (Acts 5:1-11); the wrong was the pretending, not keeping some.
exercise: Why does trust take longer to rebuild than to break? | Trust grows from many kept promises, but one lie can break it at once | Trust is rebuilt only when the other person forgets the lie | Trust never breaks at all, it only gets a little bit smaller | Trust comes back by itself after exactly one week
exercise: Tom must text Mum every time he arrives somewhere for a month. What is the best way to see it? | A ladder back to trust, one rung at a time | A punishment that proves Mum does not love him | A rule that will last for the rest of his life | A sign that Mum has forgiven him and forgotten it
exercise: What is the difference between forgiving and trusting? | Forgiving lets go of anger; trusting is relying on someone again | Forgiving and trusting are just two words for the same thing | Forgiving is for friends and trusting is only for family | Trusting comes first, and forgiving can only come afterwards
exercise: What did Peter do after he denied Jesus three times? | He went outside and wept bitterly | He ran away from Jerusalem and was never seen again | He laughed loudly and said that it did not matter | He blamed the rooster for crowing at the wrong time
exercise: How did Jesus restore Peter after the resurrection? | He asked three times "Do you love me?" and gave him work to do | He told Peter he could never be a disciple again | He made Peter say sorry in front of the whole town | He pretended the denial had never happened at all
exercise: Ruby "lost" Zayn's controller for the third time. "That's sus," says Zayn. What does "sus" mean? | Suspicious | Super cool, actually | Sorry about it | Very sensible
exercise: Why do three "losses" become a trust problem? | They make a pattern, and trust is a guess based on what someone did before | Because the third time is always the time someone is lying, as everyone knows from fairy tales | Because controllers are too expensive to lend to anybody | Because the first two losses did not matter at all
exercise: Gran forgave Grandpa's fib that week but checked the bank book for years. Is that possible? | Yes; you can forgive fully and rebuild trust step by step | No; if she really forgave him, she would never check again | No; forgiving means you must forget everything that happened | Yes, but only because the fib was about money
exercise: Callum's pal owes him "a quid" and keeps saying "the morra". What does "the morra" mean? | Tomorrow | The morning | Never | Last week
exercise: Callum says "dinnae fash" but is gutted. What is the honest thing for his pal to do? | Pay back the pound, or say honestly that he can't yet | Keep saying "the morra", because a pound is too small to matter | Avoid Callum until he forgets about the pound | Borrow another pound so that it all evens out
exercise: What did Jacob's trick cost him? | He lost his home and his brother for many years | Nothing, because he got the blessing and was happy | Only his goatskins, which he had to give back | He became blind like his father Isaac
exercise: How did the story of Jacob and Esau end? | Years later Esau ran to hug Jacob and they made peace | Esau never spoke to Jacob again for the rest of his life | Jacob tricked Esau a second time and kept everything | Isaac took the blessing back and gave it to Esau
exercise: Maya told everybody about Destiny's braces. Why does Destiny stop telling her things? | Passing on what you were told in trust breaks the trust | Because braces are something nobody should ever talk about | Because Maya told a lie about the braces | Because Destiny wanted to be friends with someone else
exercise: Which apology rebuilds trust best? | I did it, I'm sorry, I see it hurt you, I'll fix it and change | I'm sorry, but you shouldn't have made me so cross in the first place, you know | Sorry if you were upset, I didn't really do anything | Fine, sorry, can we stop talking about it now
exercise: Jacko said he'd do half the project and did "zip". In Jesus's parable of two sons, which son is Jacko like? | The one who said "I will go" and didn't go | The one who said "I won't go" and then went anyway | The one who went and worked twice as hard as his brother | The one who paid someone else to go and do the work
exercise: How did Mr Patel help Leah's dad earn back trust after he nicked sweets? | He let him work Saturdays to pay it back, and later trusted him with the till | He banned him from the shop for the rest of his life | He told him it didn't matter and gave him more sweets | He called the police and never spoke to him again
exercise: Why was Ananias and Sapphira's lie so serious if they still gave a lot? | They pretended to give everything; faking goodness was the wrong | Because they did not give enough money to the church, and the apostles wanted much more from them | Because it was against the rules to sell a field | Because they gave the money to the wrong person
test: Your brother says, "Mum forgave me, so why's she still checking my phone? Total lie, she never forgave me." What is the best answer? | She can forgive you and still need time to trust you again | He's right, forgiving means she must stop checking at once | She is checking because she secretly wants to read your messages | She is checking because she forgot that she forgave you
test: The vicar asks, "Was Peter finished after he denied our Lord?" Which reply fits the Bible best? | No; he wept, Jesus restored him, and he became a leader of the church | Yes; Jesus never spoke to Peter again after that night | Yes; Peter left the disciples and went back to fishing on the lake for good, and was never heard of again | No; because Peter's denial never really happened
test: Ellie forged Mum's signature. Now Mum signs things in front of her. Ellie asks, "How do I make her trust me faster?" What is the best advice? | Own up fully with no excuses, make amends, and be faithful in small things | Forge it again, but much more carefully this time | Wait quietly; trust comes back by itself in a day or two | Tell Mum that everyone in the class forges signatures
test: "Jacko's a total bludger, mate," says Lachlan in Perth. What is a bludger? | Someone who lets others do the work | Someone who is brilliant at sport, especially footy | Someone who tells loud, funny jokes in class | Someone who has just moved to the class from another school
test: "Dinnae fash, it's only a quid," says Callum, but he's gutted. What is really going on? | He says "don't worry", but he's upset his friend broke a promise | He doesn't care at all about the pound or about the friend, and is just saying so | He is happy because he wanted to give the pound away | He is angry about the weather in Glasgow
test: Your mate says, "Jacob got the blessing, so lying worked for him." What is wrong with that? | He lost his home and his brother for twenty years and was tricked in turn | Nothing; the story shows that tricks always pay off in the end | Jacob never lied; Esau gave him the blessing on purpose | The blessing was taken back from Jacob the very next day
test: "Shut up, you blabbed about my braces, I'm never telling you nothing again," texts Destiny. What is she upset about? | Maya passed on something told in trust, so Destiny can't trust her | Maya told lies about Destiny's teeth to the whole class and to all the teachers at school | Maya broke Destiny's braces on purpose at lunch | Maya refused to tell Destiny a secret of her own
test: What would happen if broken trust could never be mended at all? | Nobody who made a mistake could ever be relied on again, even after changing | Everybody would be much more honest and careful, and nothing would ever go wrong between friends again | Friendships would last longer because people would be careful | Nothing would change, because trust doesn't matter much
test: Josh says, "Ananias and Sapphira gave loads, so it's well harsh!" What did they actually do wrong? | They pretended to give all the money when they had kept part | They gave only half, and giving only half of the money was strictly against the church rules | They forgot to sell the field and gave nothing at all | They gave the money to Peter instead of to the poor
test: Grandpa: "I said sorry for that fib in 1983, love! Still checking the bank book, are we?" Gran laughs. What does this show? | Forgiveness can be quick while trust is rebuilt slowly | Gran never really forgave Grandpa for the fib at all, ever | Grandpa is still telling fibs about the money, even now | Bank books are more important than marriages to some people
test: Leah's dad says Mr Patel trusted him with the till a year after he nicked sweets. Why did it take a year? | Trust grows back through many kept promises, not one speech | Because Mr Patel forgot about the sweets after a year and so the problem went away by itself | Because the law says you must wait exactly one year | Because the till was broken until the next year
exam: Identify the principal distinction between forgiveness and trust, as set forth in this lesson. | Forgiveness is the relinquishing of resentment; trust is the renewed reliance upon a person, re-established gradually | Forgiveness and trust are synonymous and arise simultaneously in every instance | Trust must invariably precede forgiveness and is granted unconditionally | Forgiveness is obligatory solely in respect of trivial offences
exam: Select the statement consistent with the account recorded in John 21:15-17. | Jesus thrice asked Peter whether he loved him and thrice entrusted him with the care of his sheep | Jesus declined thereafter to address Peter in consequence of the denial, and appointed another apostle in his stead | Peter was required to make public restitution before the assembled townsfolk | Peter was appointed to a position of authority prior to his denial alone
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the deception of Isaac by Jacob (Genesis 27) is consistent with the narrative. | The deception occasioned the protracted estrangement of the brothers, reconciliation occurring only many years subsequently | The deception was immediately discovered and the blessing thereupon revoked | The deception was undertaken at the express request of Esau | The deception produced no consequences of any description for Jacob
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the wrong committed by Ananias and Sapphira (Acts 5:1-11)? | The pretence of having donated the entire proceeds whilst retaining a portion | The retention of a portion of the proceeds, retention being prohibited | The sale of land, such sales being forbidden to members of the church in Jerusalem at that time | The donation of the proceeds to persons outside the church
